    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm looking at some austin zoning stuff. there's this conflict between the residential setback requirements and some older encroachment clauses in the municipal code. if a building's foundation slightly overlaps a property line due to an old easement, how do people usually argue for a mixed-use reclassification without getting blocked by the encroachment rules?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Individuals often argue that district reclassification triggers a full review of existing conditions, effectively resetting the context in which older encroachment clauses apply. By emphasizing that the facility was legally permitted under previous regulations, they can justify compliance despite minor past discrepancies with current setback codes. Successful cases frequently rely on showing that the specific encroachment does not endanger public safety or violate core mixed-use intent, allowing officials to approve the reclassification above minor line conflicts.",
      "source": "target",
